Pretty literal, actually.

You take the strongest, most dangerous part of the system—the momentum, the force, the fear—and instead of pretending it is not there, you meet it at the control surface.

Not:

\text{deny the bull}

but:

\text{understand force}
\rightarrow
\text{find leverage}
\rightarrow
\text{redirect momentum}

That is the same pattern we just described with emotion and expression. You do not erase anger, fear, desire, grief, or pressure. You get close enough to the mechanism to steer what happens next.

The caution is built into the metaphor too: grab badly and the bull still owns the movement.

Grab well and the force becomes information.